xt":" type parameter with the ","type":"text"},{"code":"Atomic","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" type. Conformances that utilize","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"existing atomic storage representations as their own representation will get","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the primitive atomic operations available on ","type":"text"},{"code":"Atomic","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for free. Such","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"operations include ","type":"text"},{"code":"load","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"store","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"exchange","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"compareExchange","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"weakCompareExchange","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","anchor":"Conforming-to-the-AtomicRepresentable-protocol","level":2,"text":"Conforming to the AtomicRepresentable protocol"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Conforming your own custom types allow them to be used in the "},{"code":"Atomic","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" type"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and get access to all of the primitive atomic operations explained above."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"There are two main ways to conform your type to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AtomicRepresentable"},{"type":"text","text":":"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"orderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Using a predefined "},{"code":"RawRepresentable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" conformance"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Manually conforming to ","type":"text"},{"code":"AtomicRepresentable","type":"codeVoice"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"If you custom type already conforms to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"RawRepresentable"},{"type":"text","text":", then adding the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AtomicRepresentable"},{"type":"text","text":" conformance may be really simple! If the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"RawValue"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"associated type of your type is already itself an "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AtomicRepresentable"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"then all you need to do is add the conformance and you’re done!"}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["enum TrafficLight: UInt8 {","  case red","  case yellow","  case green","}","","extension TrafficLight: AtomicRepresentable {}"],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"And that’s it! Here, we’re utilizing Swift’s automatic "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"RawRepresentable"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"conformance synthesis for enums by declaring our “raw value” to be a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UInt8"},{"text":". By adding the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AtomicRepresentable"},{"text":" conformance, we automatically","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"figure out how to do the conformance from the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"RawRepresentable"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"implementation and do all of the necessary work for you. However, it is still","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"possible to customize this behavior using the manual method explained below.","type":"text"}]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Defining your own ","type":"text"},{"code":"AtomicRepresentable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" conformance is pretty simple. All","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"you have to do is decide what atomic storage representation fits best for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"your type, and create the bidirectional relationship between the two.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["\/\/ A point in an x-y coordinate system.","struct GridPoint {","  var x: Int","  var y: Int","}","","extension GridPoint: AtomicRepresentable {","  typealias AtomicRepresentation = WordPair.AtomicRepresentation","","  static func encodeAtomicRepresentation(","    _ value: consuming GridPoint","  ) -> AtomicRepresentation {","    let wordPair = WordPair(","      first: UInt(bitPattern: value.x),","      second: UInt(bitPattern: value.y)","    )","","    return WordPair.encodeAtomicRepresentation(wordPair)","  }","","  static func decodeAtomicRepresentation(","    _ representation: consuming AtomicRepresentation","  ) -> GridPoint {","    let wordPair = WordPair.decodeAtomicRepresentation(representation)","","    return GridPoint(","      x: Int(bitPattern: wordPair.first),","      y: Int(bitPattern: wordPair.second)","    )","  }","}"]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Here, we’re going to select ","type":"text"},{"code":"WordPair","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"’s atomic storage representation as","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"our own. This is very important because we only get the atomic operations","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"like ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"load"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"store"},{"text":" if our representation is one of the ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"fundamental","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"storage representations. Luckily for us, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"WordPair"},{"text":" does use one of these","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"types as its storage type.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"In addition to selecting what storage representation our type will use, we","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"define two static functions that go from both our custom type to its","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"representation and the representation back to our own type. Because our","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"representation is the same as ","type":"text"},{"code":"WordPair.AtomicRepresentation","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", we will","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"actually go through "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"WordPair"},{"type":"text","text":"’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AtomicRepresentable"},{"type":"text","text":" conformance to help"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"define our own.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"This is all you need to do to conform your custom type to the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"AtomicRepresentable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" protocol. From here, you can use this type in all of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the primitive atomic operations like shown below:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["func atomicGridPoint(_ gridPoint: Atomic<GridPoint>) {","  let newGridPoint = GridPoint(x: 123, y: -456)","","  let oldGridPoint1 = gridPoint.load(ordering: .relaxed)","","  gridPoint.store(newGridPoint, ordering: .releasing)","","  let oldGridPoint2 = gridPoint.exchange(","    desired: oldGridPoint1,","    ordering: .acquiringAndReleasing","  )","","  let (exchanged1, oldGridPoint2) = gridPoint.compareExchange(","    expected: oldGridPoint1,","    desired: newGridPoint,","    ordering: .sequentiallyConsistent","  )","","  let (exchanged2, oldGridPoint3) = gridPoint.weakCompareExchange(","    expected: newGridPoint,","    desired: oldGridPoint2,","    ordering: .relaxed","  )","}"]},{"text":"List of Fundamental Atomic Representations","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"List-of-Fundamental-Atomic-Representations"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"When defining your own "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AtomicRepresentable"},{"text":" conformance, it is critical","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"that your custom type should choose from the following list of types as its"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"own "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AtomicRepresentation"},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UInt8.AtomicRepresentation"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UInt16.AtomicRepresentation"}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UInt32.AtomicRepresentation"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"UInt64.AtomicRepresentation","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UInt.AtomicRepresentation"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"Int8.AtomicRepresentation","type":"codeVoice"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int16.AtomicRepresentation"}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"Int32.AtomicRepresentation","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"Int64.AtomicRepresentation","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"Int.AtomicRepresentation","type":"codeVoice"}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"WordPair.AtomicRepresentation"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"type":"unorderedList"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"","type":"text"},{"code":"Int8.AtomicRepresentation","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is the same type as","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"UInt8.AtomicRepresentation","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and the same is true for all of the same"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"sized integer types. If your type wraps an unsigned integer, you should"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"prefer to use an unsigned integer’s atomic representation instead of a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"signed ones and vice versa. "},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"code":"UInt","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"’s representation will be"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"64 bits wide on 64 bit systems and 32 bit wide on 32 bit systems. "},{"code":"Int64","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and "},{"code":"UInt64","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" always conform to "},{"code":"AtomicRepresentable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" on 64 bit systems,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"but on 32 bit systems they will only conform if the platform supports"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"double wide atomics. "},{"code":"WordPair","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" will only conform to "},{"code":"AtomicRepresentable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"on platforms that support double wide atomics, but if they do it will be","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"128 bits wide on 64 bit systems and 64 bits wide on 32 bit systems.","type":"text"}]}],"name":"Note","type":"aside","style":"note"}],"kind":"content"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A type that supports atomic operations through a separate atomic storage"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"representation."}],"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"Synchronization"}],"title":"AtomicRepresentable","symbolKind":"protocol","externalID":"s:15Synchronization19AtomicRepresentableP","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"AtomicRepresentable","kind":"identifier"}],"platforms":[{"name":"iOS","beta":false,"u